            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Interpolates between orientations: given key rotations (quaternions) and where they fall in time, it gives the orientation at any moment in between, turning at a steady rate along the shortest path. Slerp is spherical linear interpolation.\n\nBlending orientations number by number - np.interp, lerp or a filter on the four quaternion values - gives quaternions that are no longer proper rotations, and a turn that speeds up and slows down. slerp does it correctly. Use it to resample a recording of joint or sensor orientations to another rate, to fill a dropped frame, to blend between two poses, or to animate an object from one orientation to another.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.slerp\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nt:\nWhen to read the orientation: a number, or a list of times. Every input is read and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. Before the first key or after the last, the first or last orientation is given.\n\nquaternions:\nThe key orientations, one row of w x y z each, held. At least two.\n\ntimes:\nWhen each key happens, held. Without times the keys are at 0, 1, 2 ... - so with two keys, t from 0 to 1 goes from one to the other.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nquaternions:\nThe orientation at each t (one quaternion for a single t).\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTwo keys: no turn (1 0 0 0) and a quarter turn about z (0.7071 0 0 0.7071). A ramp from 0 to 1 slides between them; the plot shows the four values - w falling from 1 to 0.71 and z rising from 0 to 0.71, along smooth arcs (x and y stay 0).\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.rotation_mean averages orientations. quaternion_diff and quaternion_distance compare two. np.interp and scipy.cubic_spline interpolate ordinary values.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Interpolates between orientations: given key rotations (quaternions) and where they fall in time, it gives the orientation at any moment in between, turning at a steady rate along the shortest path. Slerp is spherical linear interpolation.\n\nBlending orientations number by number - np.interp, lerp or a filter on the four quaternion values - gives quaternions that are no longer proper rotations, and a turn that speeds up and slows down. slerp does it correctly. Use it to resample a recording of joint or sensor orientations to another rate, to fill a dropped frame, to blend between two poses, or to animate an object from one orientation to another.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.slerp\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nt:\nWhen to read the orientation: a number, or a list of times. Every input is read and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. Before the first key or after the last, the first or last orientation is given.\n\nquaternions:\nThe key orientations, one row of w x y z each, held. At least two.\n\ntimes:\nWhen each key happens, held. Without times the keys are at 0, 1, 2 ... - so with two keys, t from 0 to 1 goes from one to the other.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nquaternions:\nThe orientation at each t (one quaternion for a single t).\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTwo keys: no turn (1 0 0 0) and a quarter turn about z (0.7071 0 0 0.7071). A ramp from 0 to 1 slides between them; the plot shows the four values - w falling from 1 to 0.71 and z rising from 0 to 0.71, along smooth arcs (x and y stay 0).\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.rotation_mean averages orientations. quaternion_diff and quaternion_distance compare two. np.interp and scipy.cubic_spline interpolate ordinary values."
        },
